Reseats had a landing page promising two integrations and no backend behind
either. This adds both, plus the piece that makes them worth having together.

## POS API (merchant, API-key auth)

`POST /v1/pos/transactions` turns a completed sale into a receipt. Idempotent
twice over: a unique `(merchant_id, external_id)` index makes a duplicate
structurally impossible, and `Idempotency-Key` replays the original response
rather than re-running the write. Store terminals retry hard over bad wifi.

Also refunds, voids, listing, and a customer-enrollment lookup so the register
knows whether to show the prompt at all. That lookup returns a boolean and
nothing else — a merchant key must not become a way to enumerate who has a
Reseats account.

Outbound webhooks are HMAC-signed, with a transactional outbox and exponential
retry to six hours.

## Card API (customer, session auth)

Providers sit behind a `CardProvider` interface; Stripe and an in-memory mock
ship today. Stripe covers Issuing transactions and charges on saved payment
methods — it cannot feed arbitrary consumer card spend, which is exactly why
the interface exists. A bank-feed provider (Plaid, Finicity) is a new file and
one registration line.

Linking a card works backwards in time as well as forwards: receipts already
sitting unattributed with the same tender are adopted on link.

## Matching

Card transactions and POS receipts arrive out of order, minutes to days apart,
with mangled descriptors and tip-adjusted amounts. Whichever lands second binds
to the first, scored on authorization code, card fingerprint, amount (allowing
gratuity), time proximity, and normalized descriptor. Unmatched transactions
still become receipts, so non-integrated merchants are covered.

## The checkout section is now live

The "Send your receipt to Reseats too?" card was static markup. It now runs a
real sale through the same `createPosReceipt` path.

It cannot call `/v1/pos/transactions` directly — that needs a merchant API key,
and a key in browser JavaScript is a key you have given away. So there are
public `/v1/demo/*` endpoints: unauthenticated but rate limited, confined to an
auto-provisioned demo merchant, restricted to three fixed baskets. Set
`window.RESEATS_API_BASE` to point the page at a deployment; unset, it falls
back to a labelled simulation.

## Security

No PAN is stored anywhere. Cards are joined by a keyed fingerprint over brand
and last4; that fingerprint and authorization codes never leave the server. API
keys are stored as peppered HMACs, provider tokens as AES-256-GCM. The server
refuses to boot in production without `CORS_ORIGINS`.

## Deployment

`railway.json` is included — RAILPACK build, `/readyz` healthcheck, migrations
via `preDeployCommand`. Root Directory must be set to `backend` in the Railway
dashboard; Railway does not read it from config-as-code.

## Verification

118 tests pass. 31 run end to end against a real Postgres, covering idempotent
retries, matching, refund limits, webhook signing, and cross-tenant isolation.
The checkout card was driven in Chromium on both the live and fallback paths.

